@startuml serial-monitor
' How the serial monitor tells reset noise apart from a real frame.
' Grounded in serial-monitor.ps1 (Add-Bytes, Invoke-IdleFlush, Format-Bytes).

title Reset noise and a real frame, before and after

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

participant "Nucleo\n(USART2 TX)" as B
participant "ST-Link\n(USB VCP)" as S
participant "Monitor\n(PC)" as M

== Before: ReadLine, ASCII ==

B -> S : black reset button:\na few bytes, no line end
S -> M : bytes > 0x7F
note right of M
  ReadLine waits for '\n'.
  Nothing is printed yet.
end note
... 2.4 s later, the user presses the blue button ...
B -> S : equipment/0/state,on\n
S -> M : frame
note right of M
  One line, all at once:
  ?????equipment/0/state,on
  ASCII turned every byte above
  0x7F into '?', and the noise
  looks like part of the frame.
end note

== After: raw bytes, idle flush ==

B -> S : black reset button:\na few bytes, no line end
S -> M : raw bytes, kept as bytes
M -> M : quiet for 100 ms\n(IdleMs)
note right of M
  14:02:11.482  [FF FE 00 FF F8]   (no line end)
  Printed on its own line, under
  the time it actually arrived.
  Values shown, not replaced.
end note
... 2.4 s later, the user presses the blue button ...
B -> S : equipment/0/state,on\n
S -> M : frame
note right of M
  14:02:13.910  equipment/0/state,on
  Clean, under its own time.
end note

== Why 100 ms does not split a real frame ==

B -> S : equipment/0/state,off\n
S -> M : first USB chunk: equipment/0/st
S -> M : second chunk, 3 ms later: ate,off\n
note right of M
  3 ms < 100 ms, so the two chunks
  are joined into one line.
  A 21-byte frame takes about 2 ms
  on the wire at 115200 baud.
end note

legend bottom
  Every [..] is raw bytes in hex. A literal '[' or ']' in the data is
  shown as [5B] or [5D], so the brackets can never be misread.
  Times are when the PC received the bytes, not when the board sent them.
endlegend

@enduml
